# A Quantum Algorithm for the Hamiltonian NAND Tree

E. Farhi$^{1}$, J. Goldstone$^{1}$, S. Gutmann$^{2}$

$^1$ Center for Theoretical Physics, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, MA 02139.
$^2$ Department of Mathematics, Northeastern University, Boston, MA 02115.

MIT-CTP/3813 · quant-ph/0702144v2 · 22 Feb 2007

## Abstract

We give a quantum algorithm for the binary NAND tree problem in the Hamiltonian oracle model. The algorithm uses a continuous time quantum walk with a run time proportional to $\sqrt{N}$. We also show a lower bound of $\sqrt{N}$ for the NAND tree problem in the Hamiltonian oracle model.

## 1 Introduction

The NAND trees in this paper are perfectly bifurcating trees with $N$ leaves at the top and depth $n = \log_{2}(N)$. Each leaf is assigned a value $0$ or $1$; the value of any other node is the NAND of the two connected nodes just above. The goal is to evaluate the value at the root. Classically the best randomized algorithm evaluates only $N^{0.753}$ leaves in expectation (Snir 1985; Saks & Wigderson 1986), and this is tight.

There is a quantum lower bound of $\sqrt{N}$ calls to a quantum oracle. In this paper we work not in the query model but with a *Hamiltonian oracle* $H_{O}$. We present a quantum algorithm which evaluates the NAND tree in running time proportional to $\sqrt{N}$ and prove a matching $\sqrt{N}$ lower bound.

Our quantum algorithm uses a continuous time quantum walk on a graph. We start with a perfectly bifurcating tree of depth $n$ plus one additional "outer" node for each of the $N$ leaves. To specify the input we connect some of these $N$ pairs of nodes: a connection = input bit $1$; no connection = input bit $0$. We then attach a long line of nodes — the "runway" — to the root of the tree. The Hamiltonian is minus the adjacency matrix of the resulting graph; nodes are computational basis states.

We decompose $H = H_{O} + H_{D}$ where $H_{D}$ is minus the adjacency matrix of the runway + bifurcating tree (instance independent) and $H_{O}$ is minus the adjacency matrix of the leaf-to-outer-node edges (instance dependent).

**Algorithm.** Initialise in a length-$L$ right-moving packet localised on the left of the runway with right edge at $r=0$. Evolve with $H$ for time $T = L/2$. Measure the projector onto the right half of the runway ($r>0$). Output "$1$" if found, "$0$" otherwise.

The packet is chosen to be very narrowly peaked in energy around $E = 0$. With the tree attached, the transmission coefficient at $E=0$ is
$$T(0) = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{NAND tree evaluates to } 1 \\ 0 & \text{NAND tree evaluates to } 0 \end{cases}.$$
$T(E)$ stays close to $T(0)$ inside a plateau of half-width $\sim 1/(16\sqrt{N})$. Taking $L = \Theta(\sqrt{N})$ ensures the packet's spectral profile fits inside the plateau, giving run time $T = L/2 = \Theta(\sqrt{N})$.

## 2 Motion on the Runway

For $M$ so large that the runway can be treated as infinite,
$$H\lvert r\rangle = -\lvert r+1\rangle - \lvert r-1\rangle \qquad (r\neq 0). \tag{2.1}$$
Right-moving eigenstates $e^{i r\theta}$ have energy $E(\theta) = -2\cos\theta$. On the runway, the scattering eigenstates read
$$\langle r|E\rangle = e^{i r\theta} + R(E)\, e^{-i r\theta}\quad(r\le 0),\qquad \langle r|E\rangle = T(E)\,e^{i r\theta}\quad(r\ge 0). \tag{2.3–2.4}$$
Matching at $r=0$ gives $1 + R(E) = T(E)$. Defining $y(E) = \langle \text{root}|E\rangle/\langle r{=}0|E\rangle$ and applying $H\lvert r=0\rangle = -\lvert r{=}{-1}\rangle - \lvert r{=}1\rangle - \lvert\text{root}\rangle$, one obtains
$$T(E) = \frac{2 i \sin\theta}{2 i \sin\theta + y(E)}. \tag{2.9}$$
Hence $T(0) = 1 \iff y(0) = 0$ and $T(0) = 0 \iff y(0) = \infty$.

The initial state on the runway is
$$\langle r|\psi(0)\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt L}\, e^{i r\pi/2} \quad\text{for}\;-L+1 \le r \le 0,\quad 0 \text{ otherwise}. \tag{2.11}$$
This is a right-moving packet with momentum $\pi/2$ and central energy $E=0$. One checks $\langle \psi(0)|H|\psi(0)\rangle = 0$ and $\langle \psi(0)|H^{2}|\psi(0)\rangle = 5/L$; the effective spectral peak has width $\sim 1/L$ around $0$.

Evolving,
$$|\psi(t)\rangle = e^{-iHt}|\psi(0)\rangle,$$
and after $t = L/2$ measuring the right runway projector $\Pi_{>0} = \sum_{r=1}^{M} |r\rangle\langle r|$ yields the NAND-tree value with $\Omega(1)$ probability.

## 3 Calculating $y(E)$

Applying $H$ at each internal tree node yields the recursion (parent = internal node, children have subtree y-values $Y_{L}(E)$, $Y_{R}(E)$):
$$y_{\text{parent}}(E) = -E - Y_{L}(E) - Y_{R}(E),$$
with the boundary condition at each leaf: if the leaf's outer node is *not* connected (input bit $=0$) then $y_{\text{leaf}}(0) = 0$; if it *is* connected (input bit $=1$) then $y_{\text{leaf}}(0) = \infty$.

Propagating boundary values up the tree at $E=0$ recovers exactly the NAND recursion: $y_{\text{root}}(0) = 0$ if and only if the NAND tree evaluates to $1$; $y_{\text{root}}(0) = \infty$ if and only if it evaluates to $0$.

## 4 Plateau width and run-time

The paper shows $|T(E) - T(0)| < D|E|$ for $|E| < \varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon \sim 1/(16\sqrt N)$ and $D$ polynomial in $\sqrt N$. Requiring the packet's spectral support (width $\sim 1/L$) to fit inside the plateau gives $L = \Theta(\sqrt N)$, so
$$T_{\text{alg}} = L/2 = \Theta(\sqrt N).$$

## 5 Putting it all together

**Theorem.** For any NAND tree of size $N$, the continuous-time quantum walk on $G(\text{input}) = \text{runway} \cup \text{tree} \cup \text{leaf-outer edges}$ evaluates the tree correctly with probability $\ge 1/2 + \Omega(1)$ in evolution time $O(\sqrt N)$.

## 6 A lower bound for the Hamiltonian NAND tree problem

Using an adversary argument in the Hamiltonian oracle model, the paper shows that *any* quantum algorithm — including one augmented with an arbitrary instance-independent Hamiltonian $H_{A}(t)$ — needs total evolution time $\Omega(\sqrt N)$ to evaluate the NAND tree. Hence the algorithm is optimal.

## Conclusion

The paper obtains a $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ Hamiltonian-oracle quantum algorithm for the balanced binary NAND tree, quadratically faster than the classical $N^{0.753}$ query complexity, with a matching lower bound in the same model.

